From 38f06e2af922ec5b3ffa8d638c37ff6684acce5e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 19:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 73/74] nouveau/vk: an upload chunk records what the CPU wrote
 into it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 7 item 1 (CPU cost of submission)
Why: nvk_cmd_buffer_upload_alloc has two paths. The fast one carves a
 range out of cmd->upload_mem and advances used_B, which is what
 flush_mem_list cleans:
     if (mem->used_B > 0)
        nvkmd_mem_sync_map_to_gpu(mem->mem, 0, mem->used_B);
 The other takes a brand-new chunk, hands the caller offset 0 of it,
 and never touches used_B at all. If that chunk is not then adopted as
 cmd->upload_mem — which happens whenever the allocation is at least as
 large as the current upload offset — nothing ever sets used_B, the
 clean is skipped, and the bytes the CPU memcpy'd into it stay in the
 CPU's cache while the GPU reads memory.
Evidence: measured on a console 2026-09-07, vk_core/transfer section C,
 with the Horizon backend not zero-filling command memory:
     FAIL C inline update landed: 48/64 words match
     first mismatch at word 0: got 0xff97803c, want 0xa5dac00d (16 wrong)
 One cache line of a 256-byte vkCmdUpdateBuffer, arriving as heap
 rubbish. With the allocator filling, the same gap delivered that
 chunk's zeros instead, which is why nothing had ever caught it.
Upstream: yes — nothing here is Horizon-specific. The gap is in NVK's
 own bookkeeping and any backend whose mapped memory needs an explicit
 clean can be bitten by it; it is invisible on a coherent one.

One line, at the point the caller is promised [0, size) of the new
chunk. MAX2 rather than a plain assignment because used_B is a
high-water mark everywhere else it is touched, and this function is not
the place to change that.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01YajZtX9CeYzC35x2t1QJJh
---
 src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_buffer.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_buffer.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_buffer.c
index 7f373dc..871033f 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_buffer.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_cmd_buffer.c
@@ -324,6 +324,15 @@ nvk_cmd_buffer_upload_alloc(struct nvk_cmd_buffer *cmd,
    *addr = mem->mem->va->addr;
    *ptr = mem->mem->map;
 
+   /* THE CALLER IS ABOUT TO WRITE [0, size) OF THIS CHUNK, and
+    * flush_mem_list only cleans what used_B says was written. Without
+    * this line a chunk taken here and not adopted as cmd->upload_mem
+    * below keeps used_B == 0 for its whole life, so the clean is
+    * skipped and the GPU reads whatever is in memory rather than what
+    * the CPU put in the cache.
+    */
+   mem->used_B = MAX2(mem->used_B, size);
+
    /* Pick whichever of the current upload BO and the new BO will have more
     * room left to be the BO for the next upload.  If our upload size is
     * bigger than the old offset, we're better off burning the whole new
